Q: Keyboard shortcut to save file unless editor is untitled in Visual Studio Code A: <p>Try this keybinding in your <em>keybindings.json</em> file:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>  {
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+space&quot;,                       // Whatever keybinding you want
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;workbench.action.files.save&quot;,
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;editorTextFocus &amp;&amp; resourceScheme !== 'untitled'&quot;
             //  &quot;&amp;&amp; activeEditorIsDirty&quot; doesn't seem necessary
  }
</code></pre>
<p>Now, since on an untitled file this keybinding will <em><strong>not</strong></em> be triggered whatever other keybinding, you may have assigned to <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>space</kbd> will be triggered.  By default, they usually triggers suggestions. That keybinding can be disabled by adding this to your <em>keybindings.json</em> file:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>  {
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+space&quot;,
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;-editor.action.triggerSuggest&quot;,
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;editorHasCompletionItemProvider &amp;&amp; textInputFocus &amp;&amp; !editorReadonly &amp;&amp; !suggestWidgetVisible&quot;
  }
</code></pre>
<p>It may be better to choose something more unique than <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>space</kbd>.</p>

Q: How can different parts of a HTML Text node be formatted differently? A: <h3>OpenType font features</h3>
<p>The used font replaces certain character sequences via <code>calt</code> (Contextual Alternates) feature.</p>
<p>The Open Type features allow for complex group based glyph substitution.<br />
In this case the replacement rule work something like this (pseudo-code):</p>
<pre><code>[0–9](period)[0sup–9sup]
</code></pre>
<p>Worth noting: This <code>calt</code> feature usage is not very common. Unlike for more standardized features such as <code>smcp</code> (small caps: replacing all lowercase letters with small caps) you get very different replacement behaviors in each font i.e most fonts including a <code>calt</code> definition won't replace number sequences like the one from the example.</p>
<p>Besides, substitution features must be defined in the font itself – these <strong>can't be synthesized</strong> like for instance &quot;faux&quot; bolds or italics when a genuine bold or italic font variant is missing.</p>
<p>Here is a simplified reproduced example (using a customized Open Sans subset).</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>h3,
p {
  margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
}

h3 {
  font-family: sans-serif;
  font-size: 16px;
}

.price {
  font-family: 'OpenSansPrice';
  font-feature-settings: "calt" 0;
  font-size: 5em;
  color: red;
}

.calt {
  font-feature-settings: "calt" 1;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;style&gt;
  @font-face {

    font-family: 'OpenSansPrice';
    src: url('data:font/woff2;charset=utf-8;base64,') format('woff2');

    font-weight: 400;
    font-style: normal;
  }


  body {}


  h3,
  p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
  }

  h3 {
    font-family: sans-serif;
    font-size: 16px;
  }

  .price {
    font-family: 'OpenSansPrice';
    font-feature-settings: "calt" 0;
    font-size: 5em;
    color: red;
  }

  .calt {
    font-feature-settings: "calt" 1;
  }
&lt;/style&gt;


&lt;h3&gt;Calt feature disabled&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p class="price"&gt;
  4.99
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Calt feature enabled&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p class="price calt"&gt;
  4.99
&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<h3>Add features in a font editor</h3>
<p>The <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/typography/opentype/spec/features_ae#calt" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>calt</code></a> feature is often used for script-like/hand-written decorative fonts e.g to emulate a more vivid appearance: when the same letter repeats in a sequence the 2nd one is replaced by an alternative.</p>
<p>While there is a set of recommended features these are <strong>not strictly standardized</strong>. In other words: you can define replacement rules as you wish.</p>
<p><a href="https://adobe-type-tools.github.io/afdko/OpenTypeFeatureFileSpecification.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">afdko</a> is probably the most common feature definition syntax.</p>
<p>The above example was created with this <code>calt</code> rule definition :</p>
<pre><code>languagesystem DFLT dflt; # Default script
languagesystem latn dflt; # Latin script, default language
feature calt {
    # Define the glyph classes
    @numbers = [zero one two three four five six seven eight nine];
    @numbers_sup = [zero.superior one.superior two.superior three.superior four.superior five.superior six.superior seven.superior eight.superior nine.superior];

    # Substitute the target digits with superiors
    sub @numbers period @numbers' by @numbers_sup;
    sub @numbers period @numbers_sup @numbers' by @numbers_sup;
} 
calt;
</code></pre>
<p>We define 2 classes <code>@numbers</code> and <code>@numbers_sup</code> in an array syntax. The glyph names must match the ones assigned in the glyph table. These are not always standardized especially with regards to alternate glyphs. You could also assign names like <code>[zero.sup one.sup two.sup three.sup ...]</code></p>
<h3><code>calt</code> or <code>liga</code>?</h3>
<p>As previously mentioned the OpenType spec is literally pretty <em>open</em>.<br />
In fact you could put custom replacement rules in any feature you like (e.g as <code>liga</code>).</p>
<p>Ligatures are usually used to replace sequences with »monolithic« glyph design. E.g <code>f</code>, <code>f</code>, <code>i</code> with a single <code>ffi</code> glyph. (That's also why selecting characters wihtin a ligature can result in quirky caret markers. Also letter spacing usually disables ligatures completely since the renderer can't squeeze a ligature glyph design to match the letter spacing).</p>
<p>The main reason you should follow the naming conventions is most applications (graphic applications browser) have feature defaults to enable/disable certain features.</p>
<p>In modern browsers <code>calt</code> is usually enabled by default. You can toggle features in CSS via <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/font-feature-settings" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>font-feature-settings</code></a> property</p>
<pre><code>.calt {
font-feature-settings: &quot;calt&quot; 1;
}
</code></pre>
<h4>Further reading</h4>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://ilovetypography.com/OpenType/opentype-features.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">&quot;ilovetypography.com: An introduction to OpenType Substitution features&quot;</a></li>
<li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/typography/opentype/spec/featurelist" rel="nofollow noreferrer">&quot;microsoft typography: Registered Features&quot;</a></li>
</ul>

